    "project_template_level_name": "csp-unit2-lesson24-template_27_28",
    "teacher_markdown": "- Students keep the loop line `for j in range(count):` as it is. Validate looks for exactly that line.\r\n- Students write six lines: `total = 0`, the `input()` line with `int()`, the total update, `average`, and two prints. Check that these are theirs, not AI Tutor's.\r\n- Validate checks the algorithm only: that each pass uses the typed number, that `total` is the running total, that `average` is the total divided by `count`, and that the total and then the average are printed. It runs without Theater, so it does not check the picture.\r\n- If AI Tutor's pasted code changes `value` or `total`, Validate fails and says so.\r\n- If the program crashes when students run it, the error is usually in the pasted drawing code. Common causes are a color name outside the Theater palette, a negative width or height, a note outside 48 to 84, or too many pauses, since an animation is limited to 600 frames.\r\n- Common mistakes: forgetting `int()`, starting `total` inside the loop, so it resets, dividing by `j` instead of `count`, and computing `average` before the loop finishes.\r\n- Expected result for the numbers 40, 55, 30, 60, 25, 45, 35: total 290, average about 41.43.\r\n- Students will see `story_review.txt` in their files. It is for the next level, so ask students to leave it for now.\r\n- Challenge: Validate still passes if students print an extra line after the total and average.\r\n- If a student uses Build Python, they still own the sum and average. Ask them to point to the line that adds each number to the total.\r\n- Use this level to check whether students can write a running total and an average in a loop, and decide what to keep, change, or reject from AI-generated code."
  },
